<DocsLayout title="Python applications" description="Package Python as a portable Component v1 Wasm layer or a dependency-aware rootfs layer in a .holo application.">
  <h1>Python applications</h1>
  <p>Hologram can compile Python into either an import-free Component v1 <code>WasmCodemodule</code> or an architecture-specific <code>RootfsImage</code>, embed that payload in a <code>.holo</code> archive, and execute its <code>main(request: bytes) -&gt; bytes</code> function. Python remains a compiler input rather than a layer kind, so the canonical <code>.holo</code> model stays unchanged.</p>

  <div class="note"><strong>Choose the profile explicitly</strong><code>wasi-component</code> works without Docker and runs directly or resident with dependency-free projects or locked Python 3 <code>*-none-any.whl</code> packages. <code>rootfs</code> supports native/source-only dependencies such as NumPy and pandas through an experimental direct-only Docker provider. Neither profile runs host Python or reads the developer virtual environment.</div>

  <h2>What is supported</h2>
  <div class="doc-grid">
    <article class="doc-card"><h3>WASI Component · available</h3><p>Packages CPython 3.14, the application, and exact SHA-256-locked platform-independent wheels into an import-free Component v1 <code>wasm</code> layer. Direct and resident execution use the bounded Component provider.</p></article>
    <article class="doc-card"><h3>Rootfs profile · available</h3><p>Packages CPython 3.12, the application, locked Linux dependencies, compiled extensions, and shared libraries into a compressed OCI image carried by a <code>rootfs</code> layer.</p></article>
  </div>

  <h2>Requirements and contract</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>A <code>pyproject.toml</code>, a committed <code>uv.lock</code>, and a <code>src/</code> package layout.</li>
    <li><code>uv</code> for the portable compiler. The first build downloads pinned <code>componentize-py 0.25.0</code> into uv's isolated tool cache plus exact dependency wheel URLs from the lock.</li>
    <li>Python 3.12 and a Docker-compatible engine with Linux container support only for the rootfs profile.</li>
    <li>An entrypoint written as <code>module.path:function</code>. It receives one byte string and must return one byte string.</li>
    <li>A target architecture of <code>arm64</code> or <code>x86_64</code> only for rootfs. The Component profile has no <code>arch</code> or OCI <code>base</code> field.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Both profiles validate only <code>pyproject.toml</code>, the declared lock file, and <code>src/</code>; they reject absolute paths, escaping paths, and symlinks. Component compilation exposes only <code>src/</code>, a private generated adapter, and a private dependency target populated from exact lock artifacts. Rootfs compilation stages the declared project inputs and its generated launcher. The host <code>.venv</code>, Python search path, Git metadata, caches, credentials, and undeclared project files do not enter either payload.</p>

  <h2>Portable example: Python Component hello</h2>
  <p><code>examples/python-component-hello/</code> is dependency-free and compiles to the exact import-free <code>hologram:guest/component@1</code> contract. It needs no generated bindings in application source:</p>
  <pre><code># examples/python-component-hello/src/python_component_hello/__init__.py
import json
import sys


def main(request: bytes) -&gt; bytes:
    name = request.decode("utf-8").strip() or "Hologram"
    return json.dumps(&#123;
        "message": f"Hello, &#123;name&#125;!",
        "name": name,
        "python": sys.version.split()[0],
        "runtime": "python-component",
    &#125;, separators=(",", ":"), sort_keys=True).encode()</code></pre>
  <pre><code>hologram app init . \
  --template python --profile wasi-component \
  --project . --entry python_component_hello:main --lock uv.lock

hologram compile examples/python-component-hello/hologram.json \
  --output python-component-hello.holo
hologram run python-component-hello.holo \
  --input-text Ada --output-format json

# Compile once, then prove direct and resident execution
just python-component-holo-demo</code></pre>
  <p>The compiler writes a private WIT adapter, removes <code>VIRTUAL_ENV</code>, <code>PYTHONPATH</code>, <code>PYTHONHOME</code>, index overrides, and user-site lookup, and invokes pinned <code>componentize-py 0.25.0 --stub-wasi</code>. CPython and the app are inside the roughly 18.3 MiB component. The emitted layer imports no WASI, so filesystem, network, clocks, environment, arguments, stdio, DNS, secrets, and process control are absent at runtime.</p>

  <h2>Portable example: a locked dependency</h2>
  <p><code>examples/python-component-dependency/</code> declares <code>six==1.17.0</code>. Its committed lock records the exact platform-independent wheel URL and SHA-256 hash. The application imports and reports that package at runtime:</p>
  <pre><code># examples/python-component-dependency/pyproject.toml
[project]
requires-python = ">=3.14,&lt;3.15"
dependencies = ["six==1.17.0"]

# Build and run
hologram compile examples/python-component-dependency/hologram.json \
  --output python-component-dependency.holo
hologram run python-component-dependency.holo \
  --input-text Ada --output-format json</code></pre>
  <pre><code>&#123;
  "dependency": "six-1.17.0",
  "message": "Hello, Ada!",
  "name": "Ada",
  "runtime": "python-component"
&#125;</code></pre>
  <p>The compiler traverses the editable project's runtime dependency graph and ignores unreferenced development/optional records. For every reached external package, it requires a registry source and an HTTPS, SHA-256-pinned Python 3 wheel ending in <code>-none-any.whl</code>. It installs only those direct URLs using <code>--no-index --no-deps --require-hashes --only-binary :all:</code>, then rejects symlinks or native library suffixes before componentization. Git/path dependencies, source-only packages, and native wheel tags fail during <code>compile --check</code> and recommend <code>profile: "rootfs"</code>.</p>
  <div class="note"><strong>Portable boundary today</strong>Every reached runtime dependency must have a qualifying universal wheel; target-marker pruning inside that graph is deliberately conservative. Stubbed WASI bakes a build-time PRNG seed into the component, so <code>random</code> must not protect secrets and repeated clean compiles are not yet byte-identical. Secure randomness, real capability-gated WASI, deterministic component output, and a versioned build-provenance report remain follow-up work.</div>

  <h2>Rootfs example: Python hello</h2>
  <p><code>examples/python-hello/</code> is the small companion to the Wasm project example. It has no third-party dependencies, but it still carries a committed <code>uv.lock</code>, uses the schema-v2 rootfs recipe, and implements the real byte entrypoint contract:</p>
  <pre><code># examples/python-hello/src/python_hello_holo/__init__.py
import json


def main(request: bytes) -&gt; bytes:
    name = request.decode("utf-8").strip() or "Hologram"
    return json.dumps(&#123;
        "message": f"Hello, &#123;name&#125;!",
        "name": name,
        "runtime": "python",
    &#125;, separators=(",", ":"), sort_keys=True).encode()</code></pre>
  <pre><code># Validate without Docker work
hologram --json compile examples/python-hello/hologram.json --check

# Compile the project in memory and execute it
hologram run examples/python-hello \
  --input-text Ada --output-format json

# Equivalent convenience recipe
just python-hello-demo | jq .</code></pre>
  <pre><code>&#123;
  "message": "Hello, Ada!",
  "name": "Ada",
  "runtime": "python"
&#125;</code></pre>
  <p>In Desktop, add <code>examples/python-hello/</code> from <strong>Applications → Add directory</strong>. Once the Docker-backed build is Ready, choose <strong>Run</strong>, enter a name, and read the JSON response in the output panel. Existing <code>.holo</code> files can be selected with <strong>Add .holo</strong> and run the same way.</p>

  <h2>Worked example: NumPy + pandas</h2>
  <p>The repository contains the complete project under <code>examples/python-numpy-pandas/</code>. It uses the rootfs profile because NumPy contains native extensions and pandas requires NumPy. The <a href="https://numpy.org/install/">NumPy installation guide</a> recommends binary distributions when available, and the <a href="https://pandas.pydata.org/docs/getting_started/install.html#dependencies">pandas dependency guide</a> lists NumPy as required.</p>

  <h3>1. Create and lock the project</h3>
  <pre><code>uv init --app numpy-pandas-holo
cd numpy-pandas-holo
uv add numpy pandas
uv lock
uv tree</code></pre>
  <p><a href="https://docs.astral.sh/uv/guides/projects/"><code>uv add</code></a> records the dependencies in <code>pyproject.toml</code>; <code>uv.lock</code> records the exact resolved closure. Commit both files.</p>
  <pre><code>numpy-pandas-holo/
├─ pyproject.toml
├─ uv.lock
├─ request.json
├─ src/numpy_pandas_holo/__init__.py
└─ hologram.json</code></pre>

  <h3>2. Implement <code>main(request: bytes) -&gt; bytes</code></h3>
  <pre><code># src/numpy_pandas_holo/__init__.py
import json

import numpy as np
import pandas as pd


def main(request: bytes) -&gt; bytes:
    payload = json.loads(request)
    frame = pd.DataFrame(payload["rows"])
    values = frame["value"].to_numpy(dtype=np.float64)
    result = &#123;
        "columns": frame.columns.tolist(),
        "mean": float(np.mean(values)),
        "rows": int(frame.shape[0]),
        "sum": float(np.sum(values)),
    &#125;
    return json.dumps(result, separators=(",", ":"), sort_keys=True).encode()</code></pre>
  <p>The generated launcher imports the declared function, reads one request from standard input, calls it once, and writes the returned bytes to standard output. Import failures, exceptions, and non-byte return values fail the invocation.</p>

  <h3>3. Generate the manifest</h3>
  <pre><code>hologram app init . \
  --template python \
  --profile rootfs \
  --project . \
  --entry numpy_pandas_holo:main \
  --lock uv.lock \
  --arch arm64</code></pre>
  <p>Use <code>x86_64</code> on an Intel/AMD host. The command writes source-manifest schema v2:</p>
  <pre><code>&#123;
  "schema_version": 2,
  "primary": 0,
  "layers": [
    &#123;
      "kind": "rootfs",
      "source": &#123;
        "language": "python",
        "project": ".",
        "entry": "numpy_pandas_holo:main",
        "lock": "uv.lock",
        "profile": "rootfs",
        "base": "python:3.12-slim"
      &#125;,
      "arch": "arm64"
    &#125;
  ]
&#125;</code></pre>
  <p>The convenience default is <code>python:3.12-slim</code>. Tags are mutable; replace it with the registry's <code>repository@sha256:digest</code> form when reproducibility matters. Hologram validates that value but does not yet resolve or record a tag's digest automatically.</p>

  <h3>4. Validate without building</h3>
  <pre><code>hologram --json compile hologram.json --check</code></pre>
  <pre><code>&#123;
  "manifest": "hologram.json",
  "layer_count": 1,
  "schema_version": 2,
  "valid": true
&#125;</code></pre>
  <p>This parses the schema, validates the primary layer and entrypoint, and verifies that <code>pyproject.toml</code>, <code>uv.lock</code>, and <code>src/</code> exist. It does not contact the container engine or write an archive.</p>

  <h3>5. Compile a self-contained archive</h3>
  <pre><code>hologram --json compile hologram.json \
  --output numpy-pandas.holo</code></pre>
  <p>The compiler builds a clean Linux image for the selected architecture, installs the pinned <code>uv</code> build tool, runs <code>uv sync --locked --no-dev --no-editable</code>, adds the generated launcher, saves the OCI image, compresses it with Zstandard, and embeds it as a content-addressed rootfs blob. The resulting archive contains the source manifest as metadata and the canonical runtime manifest as its application identity.</p>

  <h3>6. Execute the <code>.holo</code></h3>
  <pre><code>hologram run numpy-pandas.holo --input request.json \
  --output-format json</code></pre>
  <pre><code>&#123;
  "columns": ["label", "value"],
  "mean": 20.0,
  "rows": 3,
  "sum": 60.0
&#125;</code></pre>
  <p>The runtime protocol remains binary-safe, but the CLI can decode a known application contract at the edge. Use <code>--output-format text</code> for UTF-8 or <code>--output-format json</code> for JSON. The default <code>raw</code> format retains the full result envelope, including κ, timing, resident size, and output byte arrays.</p>
  <p>Hologram ensures the verified image is available, then starts its generated entrypoint for each input with networking disabled, a read-only root filesystem, all Linux capabilities dropped, <code>no-new-privileges</code>, one CPU, 1 GiB of memory, 64 PIDs, and a 64 MiB temporary filesystem. Inputs and outputs are limited to 16 MiB and execution to 30 seconds. No project directory is mounted into the container.</p>
  <p>Bundle schema v2 records the exact Docker image ID. When that same ID is already present under the content-derived local tag, Hologram skips decompressing and loading the embedded image. A missing or mismatched image takes the cold path and is restored from the archive before execution. This preserves the self-contained archive guarantee without trusting a mutable local tag.</p>
  <p>From the repository root, <code>just python-holo-demo</code> builds the optimized release CLI and repeats this complete check against the committed example. The first invocation pays the one-time Rust release link; later invocations reuse that binary. Prefer the release CLI for large fat archives because debug-mode verification and hashing are substantially slower.</p>
  <p>The check writes exactly one versioned JSON document to standard output and sends build progress or failures to standard error, so it composes directly with <code>jq</code>:</p>
  <pre><code>just python-holo-demo | jq .
just python-holo-demo | jq '.output'

# Compile, execute, verify, and retain the archive
just python-holo-package target/numpy-pandas.holo \
  | jq '&#123;archive, archive_bytes&#125;'

# Run that same self-contained file again
target/release/hologram --json run target/numpy-pandas.holo \
  --input examples/python-numpy-pandas/request.json

# Or print only the decoded application result
target/release/hologram run target/numpy-pandas.holo \
  --input examples/python-numpy-pandas/request.json \
  --output-format json</code></pre>

  <h2>Local versus <code>mvm</code></h2>
  <p>The available provider is local OCI execution. The repository's planned <code>mvm</code> boundary currently lives in <code>hologram-sandbox</code>: its hardware-backed backend requires KVM on Linux x86_64, while its guest understands the existing Hologram graph execution protocol rather than this Python OCI/rootfs envelope. It therefore cannot run this archive today, and an Apple Silicon development host cannot use that KVM backend directly.</p>
  <p>The intended provider boundary keeps the archive stable: <code>local</code> restores or reuses the OCI image on the current machine, while a future <code>mvm</code> provider will place the same verified rootfs content behind a Linux microVM guest and warm-instance pool. Moving execution to <code>mvm</code> improves isolation and can improve warm throughput, but it does not remove the cold dependency/image build performed by <code>hologram compile</code>.</p>

  <h3>What is inside?</h3>
  <pre><code>numpy-pandas.holo
├─ AppManifest
│  └─ primary: RootfsImage · arm64 · κ(image bundle)
├─ Extension: application-directory/v1
├─ Metadata: schema-v2 source recipe
├─ ContentBlob: κ(image bundle)
│  ├─ Python OCI provider metadata
│  └─ Zstandard-compressed OCI image archive
│     ├─ CPython 3.12
│     ├─ numpy-pandas-holo
│     ├─ NumPy and compiled extensions
│     ├─ pandas and transitive dependencies
│     ├─ required Linux shared libraries
│     └─ generated Hologram launcher
└─ BLAKE3 footer</code></pre>

  <h2>Current boundaries</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>The rootfs profile supports only direct execution of a self-contained fat archive. The Python Component profile is a Wasm layer and supports <code>holo load</code>.</li>
    <li>The provider requires a local Docker-compatible engine and leaves its content-derived, image-ID-verified image in the engine cache.</li>
    <li>Container isolation is useful for a local demo, but this provider is not advertised as an untrusted or multi-tenant security boundary.</li>
    <li>Base digest resolution, byte-for-byte reproducible OCI output, build provenance, SBOMs, signatures, and the microVM provider remain milestone work.</li>
    <li>Python Component v1 runs dependency-free projects and locked platform-independent wheels directly and resident. Native/source-only packages require rootfs; deterministic Component output and any real capability-gated WASI profile remain planned.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>See the <a href={sitePath('/docs/holo-files')}><code>.holo</code> format guide</a>, <code>specs/adrs/008-python-rootfs-oci-provider.md</code>, <code>specs/adrs/012-locked-python-component-dependencies.md</code>, and <code>specs/plans/holo-application-runtime.md</code> for the archive contract, provider decisions, and remaining milestone checklist.</p>
</DocsLayout>
